<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacobin Club</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a>–<a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Jacobins</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jacobin Club was the most influential political club of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, named after the former Dominican convent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> where it met, the Dominicans being known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as Jacobins. Under Robespierre it became the centre of radical republicanism and backed the Reign of Terror of 1793–94, and it was closed after his fall. The name Jacobins was later used across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for radical republicans and supporters of the French revolutionary cause.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacobin-club.jpg" alt="Jacobin Club" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author Vector redesign by VectorVoyager · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Jacobins_of_Paris_(Republican).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
